# === Required (build-time, baked into client bundle) ===
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# === Required (runtime, server-side only) ===
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# === Optional (build-time) ===
# Public URLs the apps are served on. Player at the apex, admin under /admin.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://sfubadminton.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAYER_URL=https://sfubadminton.com
# The console's public base URL, path prefix INCLUDED. Self-referential
# redirects are built from it, and its origin (path dropped) is what the
# WebAuthn check compares against — so the /admin belongs here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_URL=https://sfubadminton.com/admin
# Where the console is mounted. basePath is baked into the Next build, so this
# is build-time only — setting it in the Pi's runtime .env does nothing. Must
# match the `proxy.path` label on the admin service and the /admin the player
# app's Exec Panel links to. Empty = root-mounted (localhost).
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH=/admin
# Domain a passkey is scoped to. Keep this the PARENT domain so one credential
# works on the apex and on any subdomain — and survives the console moving
# between them. Deriving it from the console's host instead invalidates every
# enrolled key, and admins then cannot reach the page that enrols a new one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PASSKEY_RP_ID=sfubadminton.com
# Domain the Supabase auth session cookie is scoped to. LEAVE IT EMPTY: the
# console now shares the player app's origin, so a plain host-only cookie
# already covers both and there is nothing to widen. (It exists for the case
# where the console lives on a subdomain, where ONE sign-in covering both needs
# ".sfubadminton.com"; empty is also the only thing that works on localhost,
# where some browsers reject a ".localhost" domain.)
#
# Build-time, and it must be the same for BOTH images: the browser writes this
# cookie on every token refresh, so a server/browser or player/admin mismatch
# creates two cookies of the same name on different scopes. Setting it only in
# the Pi's runtime .env does nothing — it has to be a build arg.
NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=

# === Optional (runtime) ===
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_EMAIL=
SENTRY_DSN=
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Signs the one-click unsubscribe links in notification email. Generate:
# openssl rand -base64 48
#
# UNSET IS SILENT AND IT IS NOT SAFE. buildUnsubscribeUrl throws when the secret
# is missing, safeUnsubscribeUrl catches that and returns null, and BOTH halves
# of the unsubscribe affordance disappear from every one of the ten senders: no
# List-Unsubscribe / List-Unsubscribe-Post header for Gmail's one-click control,
# AND no "Turn off these emails" footer link for the person reading it. Nothing
# fails, nothing logs, and the mail still sends — so the first symptom is a
# member reaching for "Report spam" because there is nothing else to click.
#
# The domain those complaints land on is the one that also delivers SIGN-IN
# CODES (noreply@ and login@ are both mail.sfubadminton.com), so a deliverability
# problem here turns into "nobody can log in".
#
# Set it ONCE and keep it: the tokens carry no timestamp and there is no token
# table, so rotating this invalidates every unsubscribe link ever mailed. Both
# apps need the same value — admin mints links when the cron jobs send, player
# mints and verifies them.
EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=
# Shared secret every scheduled job presents. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32.
#
# Also silent when unset or mismatched: the Next cron routes and the edge
# functions both fail CLOSED with a 401 (correct), the Pi's crontab discards the
# status, and pg_cron records the SQL as `succeeded` regardless. Reminders,
# digests and expiry sweeps simply stop, with no log, no mail and no Sentry
# event anywhere.
#
# The SAME VALUE has to be in three unsynchronised places — this file's .env
# (the player and admin containers), the edge runtime's function secrets, and
# `cron_config.reminder_secret` in the database. Rotating one silences a class
# of notifications with no signal.
CRON_SECRET=
# ARN of the SNS topic SES publishes bounces and complaints to. The webhook at
# /admin/api/webhooks/ses REJECTS everything while this is unset — a valid
# Amazon signature only proves a message came from SNS, not from a topic we
# own, so an unset ARN would leave anyone able to suppress any member's address.
# Set this BEFORE creating the SNS subscription, or the confirmation 403s.
SES_SNS_TOPIC_ARN=
# Svix signing secret for the Resend bounce/complaint webhook, in the form
# whsec_<base64>. Copy it from the Resend dashboard when you create the endpoint
# pointing at /admin/api/webhooks/resend, subscribed to email.bounced and
# email.complained.
#
# UNSET IS SILENT IN THE WORST DIRECTION. The route answers 503 without it, so
# Resend retries for a while and then gives up, and every hard bounce and spam
# complaint is discarded — which is exactly the state production has been in
# since email_suppressions was created: the table is EMPTY, and the club has
# been mailing addresses that permanently rejected it and people who pressed
# "report spam". Nothing anywhere records either, and the first symptom of the
# reputation damage is legitimate mail silently stopping for everybody.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Signs the admin passkey-gate cookies. Generate: openssl rand -base64 48
# Signs the short-lived WebAuthn challenge cookie. TWO separate values: each app
# signs its own cookie, so neither can replay the other's challenge.
#
# The player one was missing from this file for a long time, and therefore from
# the deployment — which does not fail loudly. Passkey sign-in and enrolment in
# the PLAYER app simply answer 503, so a member sees "Passkeys are not
# configured" and assumes their device is at fault. Generate each with
# `openssl rand -base64 48`.
ADMIN_PASSKEY_COOKIE_SECRET=
PASSKEY_COOKIE_SECRET=

# === Pi deploy (proxy-manager routing) ===
# Hostname the proxy serves this stack on (the club domain). BOTH apps are on
# it: the player at "/", the console at "/admin". proxy-manager matches the
# longest path prefix first, so /admin wins over the player's catch-all.
#
# There is no ADMIN_PROXY_DOMAIN any more — the console has no subdomain. See
# the admin service's proxy.* labels in docker-compose.yml.
PROXY_DOMAIN=sfubadminton.com
# Mount point for the console. Only read by a fallback `docker compose --build`
# on the Pi; CI passes it from the workflow matrix. Must match NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_PATH.
ADMIN_BASE_PATH=/admin

# === Discord bot ===
# DISCORD_APPLICATION_ID, DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY, DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN,
# DISCORD_SERVICE_SECRET and APP_API_URL are required for the `bot` service to
# start: the compose files use the ${VAR:?} form so compose refuses to come up
# rather than booting a bot that rejects every interaction with an unexplained
# signature error. DISCORD_GUILDS and DISCORD_AUDIT_CHANNEL_ID are optional --
# the guild map normally comes from the database (migration 00167); the env var
# is only the bootstrap path for before that migration is applied.
#
# NOTE these are plain .env values, NOT the dashboard's ref:NAME secret refs.
# ref: is resolved server-side by proxy-manager only for env sent to its API
# (replace_service / stage_canary / onboarding); compose does no such
# resolution and would set the literal string.
#
# From discord.com/developers -> your application:
#   General Information -> Application ID, Public Key
#   Bot -> Reset Token
DISCORD_APPLICATION_ID=
DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY=
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
# Shared secret proving a request on /api/discord/* came from the bot. The SAME
# value must reach the `player` service and the `discord_service_secret` row in
# cron_config (see 00166). Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
DISCORD_SERVICE_SECRET=
# guild id -> role name -> role id, as one line of JSON. Role names must be
# exactly the nine in apps/bot/src/roles.ts MANAGED_ROLES; parseGuildRegistry
# rejects an unknown name at boot instead of silently skipping that role.
# {"<guild_id>":{"linked":"...","session_staff":"...","vp":"...","executives":"...","competitive":"...","recreation":"...","internal":"...","alumni":"...","external":"..."}}
DISCORD_GUILDS=
# OPTIONAL. Channel id the bot posts an audit embed to after every action it
# takes: each /unlink, each link, and one rolled-up entry per nightly sweep.
# Leave blank to disable - the bot logs which mode it is in at startup. Make it
# a private channel: the entries mention the members whose roles changed, and
# the bot needs View Channel + Send Messages there.
DISCORD_AUDIT_CHANNEL_ID=
# Where the bot reaches the player app's /api/discord/* routes. REQUIRED, with
# no default, and the reason is worth reading before you pick a value.
#
# This used to default to http://player:3000. That is wrong in every
# environment we actually run: player and admin are onboarded through the proxy
# dashboard, not started by compose, so they run under generated names
# (goproxy-onb-<service>-1000 on staging, goproxy-badminton-player-NN on prod)
# and no container or network alias named `player` exists. The default resolved
# to NXDOMAIN, and every call surfaced as `TypeError: fetch failed` -- which
# reads as a network fault rather than as the misconfiguration it was.
#
# Two values work. Pick deliberately:
#
#  1. The in-cluster container name, e.g.
#       APP_API_URL=http://goproxy-onb-badminton-staging-player-1000:3000
#     Fastest, stays on the edge network, never touches the internet. But the
#     name is generated by the dashboard: stable for a single-replica onboarded
#     service, NOT stable on prod where replicas are numbered and increment.
#     Confirm with `docker ps` after any replace, and re-check it after scaling.
#
#  2. The public origin, e.g.
#       APP_API_URL=https://badminton.polardev.org
#     Immune to container renames, so it survives replaces and scaling untouched.
#     Costs an egress round trip through Cloudflare for what is a same-host call,
#     and it inherits the edge: if maintenance mode is on, the bot is down too,
#     because there is no bypass for it (the bypass keys on the raw TCP peer,
#     which is Cloudflare here).
#
# Prefer (1) on staging where the name is stable, (2) on prod unless you are
# prepared to re-point this on every scale event.
APP_API_URL=
# The origin used to BUILD the /link button a member clicks, so it must be the
# public one even when APP_API_URL is not - a container name is unreachable
# from someone's phone.
APP_PUBLIC_URL=
